"""Now we can read it in using [`.read_csv()'](https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/reference/api/pandas.read_csv.html). Some important arguments:
* filepath
* `sep`: if not "," --- typical alternatives are semicolons ";" or tabs "\t" --- applicable also for saving files using the `.to_csv()` method.
* `header`: assumes by default that the initial row is a header. Specify None for no header, or a list for multiple header rows!
* `index_col`: similarly to header, which columns to use as index. By default, none.
* `usecols`: to use only a subset of the columns
* `skiprows`: number of, or list of line numbers to skip (indexed from 0)
* `nrows`: number of rows to read in (useful for large files)
*  `parse_dates`: the list of column names pandas should try to parse as dates
* `decimal`: "." by default, but sometimes you may want to use ","
* `quotechar`: by default ", which delimts the cells
* `encoding`: the character encoding to use -> problems with reading in files often arise from bad choice of encoding, although nowadays most text is utf-8
    * refer to [list of encodings](https://docs.python.org/3/library/codecs.html#standard-encodings)

In our case, since we saved the .csv file using the default settings (regular separator and quote character), we can use the default settings to load it:
"""

df_loaded = pd.read_csv("data.csv")

df_loaded

"""With some fancy arguments:"""

pd.read_csv("data.csv", index_col=0, nrows=3, skiprows=[2, 3], parse_dates = ["year"])

"""Encoding:"""

df_loaded.iloc[0,1] = "árvíztűrő tükörfúrógép"

df_loaded.to_csv("data2.csv", index=False, encoding="iso-8859-2")

try:
    display(pd.read_csv("data2.csv"))     ## uses UTF-8 encoding by default
except Exception as e:
    print(f"Exception ({type(e).__name__}):", e)

display(pd.read_csv("data2.csv", encoding="iso-8859-2"))

"""Separator:"""

df_loaded.to_csv("data3.csv", index=False, sep = ";")

display(pd.read_csv("data3.csv"))

display(pd.read_csv("data3.csv", sep=";"))

